## Tool Timeouts vs Slow Kernel Work

Each tool call that executes kernel code has a hard per-call timeout (typically 60 seconds; the exact value per tool is in that tool's rules file). If a kernel operation will take longer (combined_data rebuild ~150s in eod_option_algos, eod_355_option_algos, big SQL pull, large file load, full backtest sweep, etc.), do NOT try to run it through the tool -- the cell gets interrupted and the kernel state is left in a partial/broken spot.

Also do NOT spin up a parallel subprocess to do the work outside the kernel just because the kernel can't fit it in the timeout -- the user did not ask for a 3-minute background task and won't expect one.

Correct pattern:

Recognize the work is too slow for the tool call.
Tell the user: "this will exceed the tool's 60s timeout -- can you run `<exact snippet>` in your kernel and ping me when done?"
Wait. Do not start parallel rebuilds, subprocess pickles, scheduled wakeups, or anything else.
When the user confirms "done", continue from the now-populated kernel state.
Default to asking, not doing, whenever the task plausibly crosses the timeout line.

